  "read": {
    "headline": "Oil gave up the premium, the long end took it, and the Nasdaq closed at a record.",
    "standfirst": "Monday was a risk day with one exception. The S&P 500 rose 1.49% to 7,764.70, the Nasdaq Composite 2.26% to 27,122.09 for its first record close since 2 June, and the Dow 0.71% to 52,048.83, while WTI fell to an eleven-day low and Brent settled at $100.34. The ten-year closed at 4.96%, back under 5% after touching 5.041% last week, and the two-year did not move at all. Energy was the only sector down. The one-year column, blank across most of this board yesterday, is carried by the source table again today.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Ten of the eleven sectors rose. The eleventh was the one that has led all year.",
        "text": "Energy fell 2.88% on the day while technology rose 2.77% and communication services 3.56%. Energy is still up 39.70% year to date and 42.47% over twelve months, the best sector on both windows — it is giving back the war premium, not breaking down. Crude is down 8.26% over five sessions and gasoline 8.32%, and the equity sector has now moved with it rather than lagging as it did last week."
      },
      {
        "title": "The ten-year came back under five and the two-year did not move.",
        "text": "The ten-year yield is 4.96% against 5.01% on Friday, a 1.00% fall on the day, and the thirty-year is 5.29% after a 0.94% fall. The two-year was unchanged at 4.76%. That takes 2s10s to 20bp from 25 and 3M10Y to 79bp from 87: the long end rallied on the oil move while the front end stayed where the Fed put it. Flattening from the long end is a different message from flattening from the front, and this week's three auctions will test it."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea's 4.33% day is partly Korea and partly the won.",
        "text": "The Korea ETF rose 4.33% and Taiwan 3.58%, the two largest moves on the equity board, and both are priced in dollars. USD/KRW fell 1.40% on the day and 11.37% over three months, so roughly a third of Monday's Korean move is currency. On the twelve-month column Korea is up 131.93% and Taiwan 80.63%, against 15.99% for the United States. Whatever this is, it is not a broad global rally."
      },
      {
        "title": "The one-year column is back, and it is an energy column.",
        "text": "The source table carried no one-year figures for most of the board yesterday; today it does. Heating oil is up 106.52% over twelve months, gasoline 62.55%, wheat 58.04% and Brent 53.01%. Gold is up 15.68% over the same year and silver 49.52%. An inflation the metals only half-tracked is what the last twelve months have been, and it is the reason a ten-year at 4.96% is being called low."
      },
      {
        "title": "The biggest single move on this board is a pork contract.",
        "text": "Lean hogs fell 10.63% in one session and 11.53% over five, more than anything in crypto, equities or energy. Over three months they are down 28.21% and over twelve 29.35%. It is on the board because a board about what moved should show what moved — not because it belongs in anybody's macro thesis."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crypto had the week and sold the day equities rallied.",
        "text": "Dogecoin is up 21.47% over five sessions, Cardano 20.40%, XRP 16.46% and Solana 14.66%, yet every major coin fell on Monday — Bitcoin 1.46%, Ethereum 1.65%, Solana 1.97% — on a day the Nasdaq closed at a record. Chainlink at 2.56% is the only one positive year to date; Cardano is down 70.51% over twelve months. The five-day column and the one-year column are describing different assets."
      }
    ]
  },
